            <div xml:id="d406">

                <pb facs="0587" n="511" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg511"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.151/125</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Greece</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 307</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Athens</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-07-12">July 12, 1934</date>.<lb/>[Received August
                        14.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In reference to the Department’s telegram
                    No. 42 of July 6, 3 p.m. and my reply No. 70 of July 11, 10 a.m., I have the
                    pleasure to report that during my absence from Athens on a leave of short
                    duration, the matter in question was handled by Mr. Gade, Secretary of Legation,
                    acting as Chargé d’Affaires a. i. He requested and obtained <pb facs="0588"
                        n="512" xml:id="pg512"/> a personal interview with the Foreign Minister, as
                    suggested in the Department’s telegram, and secured the postponement desired
                    without the necessity of invoking the serious view which the American Government
                    would take of any arbitrary action by the Greek Government at this time.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lincoln MacVeagh</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
